INT. SCHOOL BUS DEPOT, MAINTENANCE PIT - LATE AFTERNOON

Oil, rust, the bones of old buses. Fluorescent bars hum overhead. Work lights hang from a rolling cart.

CLARENCE "CLAY" ABERNETHY-OKORO (50s, coveralls, a pencil behind each ear) lies on a creeper beneath a bus, wrenching at something unseen. A radio on the floor plays low country.

Footsteps on concrete. Sneakers. A teenage girl hovers at the edge of the pit, backpack on one shoulder, staring down.

CLAY
(from under the bus)
Is that you, Wren?

The girl is WREN ABERNETHY-OKORO (16, hair in a messy braid, headphones around her neck). She sits at the edge of the pit, feet dangling.

WREN
Hi, Dad.

CLAY
You're early.

WREN
I walked.

CLAY
It's four miles.

WREN
I know how far it is.

A wrench clanks. Clay rolls out on the creeper, wiping his hands on a rag, and looks up at his daughter. His face softens, then tightens as he sees hers.

CLAY
What happened?

WREN
Nothing.

CLAY
Wren.

WREN
I quit the team.

He sits up slowly. The radio sings about a dog and a truck.

CLAY
Okay.

WREN
Aren't you going to yell?

CLAY
Do you want me to?

WREN
Kind of.

CLAY
Then I will. (beat) Wren Abernethy!

WREN
(smiling despite herself)
That's not yelling.

CLAY
I'm out of practice.

She slides down into the pit and sits on the creeper next to him. He hands her a wrench. She turns it over in her hands.

WREN
Dad? I'm okay, you know.

CLAY
I know, kiddo.

WREN
Can I help?

CLAY
Hold the light.
